  "companyID": "58c62e995865713647a7115d",
  "created": "2019-06-12T04:19:14.133Z",
  "deletedAt": null,
  "details": "It seems obvious that creators value the freedom to create anything their heart desires. When it comes to avatars, sometimes their creation has to deal with the constraints of poly limitations, drawcalls, dynamic bones, shaders, and other additions. What if the game allowed a way where users can have full creative freedom in creating avatars. For the sake of terminology I will call avatars with highpoly, a lot of drawcalls, heavy shaders, or a huge amount of dynamic bones,  high-fidelity avatars. What if we gave users, perhaps only Trusted Users, the ability to upload high-fidelity avatars entrusting them that they will use them responsibly. These can be avatars that can't be favorited or enabled to be used on avatar pedestals but perhaps can be allow for cloning. Developers for VRChat can continue to push for more optimized avatars while also having this feature be part of the VRChat experience. When developing a high-fidelity avatar I want to think that there are little to no restrictions aside from not being anything nsfw. I want to think to upload a high-fidelity avatar, we could have a separate enable button in the publish interface before making a build of our avatar to upload to VRChat. It could be located in the middle of the Description field above Content Warnings. We could also have an enable button for \"I am a responsible trusted user\" if we go with the idea that only trusted users can upload high-fidelity avatars. Perhaps a profile Trusted User check can be done when uploading to one's VRChat account or before publishing the avatar when it logs a person in within Unity. Overall, I think by allowing for high-fidelity avatars, players in VRchat can use these avatars whenever they are in small or private groups. It also provides a reward and benefit for reaching a Trusted User rank. Nevertheless, what is most important is that it allows for more freedom to create unique and interesting avatars. ",
